WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:20.246 --> 00:00:24.433
The algorithms that allow us to
reveal your intimate traits and

00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:29.727
psychological profile, one of the powerful
application of such algorithms,

00:00:29.727 --> 00:00:34.625
is to target you with marketing messages
and tailor those messages to make

00:00:34.625 --> 00:00:39.570
them most interesting and
most relevant to you as an individual.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:43.986
Now, one of the types of
marketing that is heavily

00:00:43.986 --> 00:00:48.730
employing such approaches
is political marketing.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:53.429
And in fact, Barack Obama was one of
the first politicians that started

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:57.420
using algorithms of this
kind on a massive scale.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:02.470
And now, it's a fact

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:07.560
of life that politicians will use
micro-targeting of their political

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.420
messages to reach out to the voters.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.390
Naturally, people are concerned about it.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:20.213
But my personal opinion is that
people are concerned about the wrong

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.910
part of the story here.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:27.492
So just the sheer fact that you can
target people with relevant messages,

00:01:27.492 --> 00:01:32.444
messages that are matched to
their interests, fears, dreams,

00:01:32.444 --> 00:01:36.750
personalities, and so on,
is a very good thing.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:41.826
That is an equivalent of a politician
coming to your household,

00:01:41.826 --> 00:01:47.190
taking time to get to know each one
of the members of your household.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:52.484
And then trying to explain to them,
hey, you see my program, this part

00:01:52.484 --> 00:01:58.306
of my program or that part of my program,
will help you to realize your dreams,

00:01:58.306 --> 00:02:04.610
and prevent the bad things that you fear
from happening, which is an amazing thing.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:07.150
It keeps the message
more relevant to people.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:10.650
It keeps people more
interested in the message.

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:15.870
And it also makes people more competent
to judge the quality of such message.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.390
Which is great for democratic process.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:23.390
We're having more engaged,
more interested voters that

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:29.733
are being talked to about things that
are important and relevant to them.

00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:32.118
So just great news for democracy.

00:02:32.118 --> 00:02:33.230
I believe.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:37.144
Also, it has also many other consequences
that are very often overlooked.

00:02:37.144 --> 00:02:42.380
If I can target voters with my
message in an accurate way,

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:47.072
I basically can stop broadcasting,
just generate

00:02:47.072 --> 00:02:52.550
messages using TVs, and
expensive ads in newspapers.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:55.210
Which basically means that political

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.920
dialogue between politicians and
voters became much cheaper.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.300
I can reach the voters
using way cheaper means,

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:08.930
such as Facebook marketing targeting,
or adverts, or

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.900
Tweets and adverts, or
adverts sending other environments.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:14.710
Which, again,

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:20.750
has an amazing effect of decreasing
the reliance of politicians on big money.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:27.370
If you can be talking to voters
at a fraction of a cost that you

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.960
needed 10 or 20 years ago, it means that
you also have much less debts to pay

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:39.250
to big industry and other potentially
very influential players on the market.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.474
So it's great news, and

00:03:40.474 --> 00:03:43.929
we had at least two big examples of
that happening in recent election.

00:03:43.929 --> 00:03:50.270
We had Bernie Sanders and Donald Trump who
both were not the part of establishment.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:55.880
They both didn't have much
experience in politics,

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.180
really, or kind of running for
President politics.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:04.195
And also they have had very small
funds compared with funds that

00:04:04.195 --> 00:04:08.520
some other mainstream politicians
like Jeb Bush or Hillary Clinton had.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:13.547
And yet, they were able to
energize great numbers of voters,

00:04:13.547 --> 00:04:18.196
and in the case of one of them,
win the election, right?

00:04:18.196 --> 00:04:22.548
Another, I think, consequence
of micro-targeting in political

00:04:22.548 --> 00:04:27.129
messaging that people are often
overlooking is that because you are not

00:04:27.129 --> 00:04:30.260
limited to broadcasting generic messages,
and

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:35.780
you can now micro-target messages to make
them interesting and relevant to people.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:40.486
What happens is that now it makes sense
to reach out to the voters that were

00:04:40.486 --> 00:04:43.740
previously excluded from
the political dialog.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.517
Because maybe we didn't make an economic
sense to try to target them with

00:04:48.517 --> 00:04:50.410
your broadcasted message.

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:56.100
And again, we can see that with
both Trump and Sanders energizing

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:01.720
types of electorate that was
not really energized before.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:06.460
The larger the fraction of the society
that we bring in to the political process,

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.750
the better for democracy.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:18.010
Now, having said that, you can use the
very same political message targeting to,

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:22.620
let's say, discourage people from voting.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.042
And we have seen this in
last selection as well,

00:05:26.042 --> 00:05:31.178
where you could see very well targeted
messages aimed at the electorate

00:05:31.178 --> 00:05:35.900
of your opponent to try to discourage
them from casting the vote.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:42.010
And now,
this is an awful use of such technology.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:46.870
But again, instead of focusing on
trying to blame the technology for

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:52.420
this thing happening, we should rather
kind of, technology's just a medium here.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.710
We should just focus on
the phenomenon itself.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:06:00.066
We should perhaps start discussing
whether it's okay to allow for

00:06:00.066 --> 00:06:02.330
negative marketing in politics.

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:07.390
And by the way, there are countries,
the free Western democracies

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:13.720
where negative marketing in politics is
basically not acceptable or even illegal.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:18.703
[MUSIC]

00:06:18.703 --> 00:06:22.547
Human history is a history
of great change, full stop.

00:06:22.547 --> 00:06:25.750
We were always living
through great changes.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:30.610
It's just that recently,
the speed of progress has accelerated.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:36.120
So we have a great change every
couple months, or every couple years.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.230
But we also have a lot of evidence
from the history showing that humanity

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:45.200
basically adjusts their behavior,
and figures out how to

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:50.570
avoid the downsides, and employ
the upsides of a given great change.

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:53.856
And starting from agriculture
through industrial revolution.

00:06:53.856 --> 00:06:58.077
And then, something perhaps
more relevant to the subject of

00:06:58.077 --> 00:07:01.812
this conversation which is
explosion of marketing.

00:07:01.812 --> 00:07:06.365
When you first had
billboards on the roads, or

00:07:06.365 --> 00:07:13.504
first large format ads in newspapers,
people were also discussing it.

00:07:13.504 --> 00:07:16.588
It was seen as the end of
the world as we know it, and

00:07:16.588 --> 00:07:18.802
now everyone will be manipulated.

00:07:18.802 --> 00:07:23.876
And the world will be just handed over
to marketers who'd be just running it.

00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:29.150
And we kind of know that
is just did not happen.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:33.800
Partly, because people developed
an immune system, immunity to those ads.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.359
And now, l think that the same will
happen with digital marketing.

00:07:36.359 --> 00:07:41.054
You probably already stopped
noticing adverts on the websites,

00:07:41.054 --> 00:07:45.590
your brain learned it's
just not worth looking at.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:49.340
Not to mention,
that now we have technology, ad blockers,

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:56.230
that just basically explicitly remove
those ads from your online experience.

00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:00.710
Now obviously, the progress,
it's a never ending race.

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:07.484
So once we learn how to avoid being
swayed too much by online ads,

00:08:07.484 --> 00:08:16.370
the companies are just developing new
ways of manipulating or influencing us.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:22.880
And a good example is related to
how news feed on Facebook works.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:29.072
So people don't realize that when they see
a story that is shared by their friend,

00:08:29.072 --> 00:08:34.830
the fact that they see a story is a
product of both friends sharing the story.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:38.181
But also, the company behind the story,

00:08:38.181 --> 00:08:42.588
the outlets behind the story
paying Facebook money for

00:08:42.588 --> 00:08:47.110
this given story being shown
on your news feed, right?

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:48.752
So people don't really realize,

00:08:48.752 --> 00:08:51.810
people think,
it's just my friend sharing a story.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:56.705
And what I can see here on my news feed
is a representative representation of

00:08:56.705 --> 00:08:59.047
what my friends are talking about.

00:08:59.047 --> 00:09:00.527
In fact, it's not.

00:09:00.527 --> 00:09:05.725
It's a product of what your friends are
talking about and how much money a given

00:09:05.725 --> 00:09:11.100
company behind a given story was
willing to pay Facebook to promote it.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:15.914
And I think that people don't
realize it now, but with time,

00:09:15.914 --> 00:09:20.266
we'll just adjust our
perception to this phenomenon,

00:09:20.266 --> 00:09:24.630
as we adjusted our perception
to phenomena of the past.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:27.989
[MUSIC]

